{"id": "section-body-23664", "title": "Varicocele", "chapterId": "chapter-twenty-one", "tocLevel": 4, "tocPath": ["APPROACH TO PATIENTS WITH EMERGENCY CONDITIONS", "KIDNEY AND UROLOGICAL DISORDERS", "Urology Disorders", "Varicocele"], "blocks": [{"id": "body-23664", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Varicocele", "runs": [{"text": "Varicocele"}], "level": 4, "list": {"level": 2, "numberingId": "541"}}, {"id": "body-23665", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Introduction Varicocele is the abnormal dilation of the pampiniform venous plexus within the spermatic cord. It is most common on the left side (90%) due to the anatomical entry of the left renal vein. It is a significant cause of preventable male infertility and testicular atrophy.", "runs": [{"text": "Introduction"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Varicocele"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "abnormal"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "dilation"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "pampiniform"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "venous"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "plexus"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "within"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "spermatic"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "cord."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "It"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "most"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "common on the left side (90%) due to the anatomical entry of the left renal vein. It is a significant cause of preventable male infertility and testicular atrophy."}]}, {"id": "body-23667", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Clinical Presentation", "runs": [{"text": "Clinical"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Presentation"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-23668", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Usually asymptomatic but may present as a \"bag of worms\" sensation or a dull, aching pain in the scrotum that worsens with standing. Physical Examination: Should be performed in both standing and supine positions. Use the Valsalva Maneuver to accentuate dilated veins.", "runs": [{"text": "Usually asymptomatic but may present as a \"bag of worms\" sensation or a dull, aching pain in the scrotum that worsens with standing. Physical Examination: Should be performed in both standing and supine positions. Use the Valsalva Maneuver to accentuate dilated veins."}]}, {"id": "body-23669", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Grading: Grade 1: Palpable only with Valsalva.", "runs": [{"text": "Grading:"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Grade"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "1:"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Palpable"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "only"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "with"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Valsalva."}]}, {"id": "body-23670", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Grade 2: Palpable without Valsalva but not visible. Grade 3: Visible through the scrotal skin.", "runs": [{"text": "Grade"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "2:"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Palpable"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "without"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Valsalva"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "but"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "not"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "visible. Grade 3: Visible through the scrotal skin."}]}, {"id": "body-23673", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Differential Diagnosis", "runs": [{"text": "Differential"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Diagnosis"}]}, {"id": "body-23674", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "Hydrocele: Transilluminates (Varicocele does not).", "runs": [{"text": "Hydrocele:"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Transilluminates"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(Varicocele"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "does"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "not)."}], "list": {"level": 0, "numberingId": "538"}}, {"id": "body-23675", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "Inguinal Hernia: Reducible mass with a cough impulse.", "runs": [{"text": "Inguinal"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Hernia:"}, {"text": " "}